Spare underwear is another useful item. An unexpected leak can happen, and having a clean pair available can make the situation much less stressful.

Tissues can also be useful when changing a pad. A small pack takes up little space and can be kept in your bag.

A small disposal pouch or reusable waterproof bag can help you carry used items until you find an appropriate disposal bin. Used sanitary pads should be wrapped properly and disposed of in a suitable waste bin.

Hand sanitiser can be useful when you do not have immediate access to soap and water. However, whenever possible, wash your hands properly before and after changing a menstrual product.

You may also want to keep a small bottle of drinking water with you. Staying hydrated is important throughout the day.

If you normally experience menstrual cramps, you can discuss suitable pain-management options with a healthcare professional or pharmacist and carry any appropriate medication according to the instructions.

A small zippered pouch can keep all your supplies organised. Choose something discreet and easy to carry.

Your period emergency kit does not need to be large. A few sanitary pads, spare underwear, tissues, disposal bags, and basic hygiene supplies may be enough.

Check your kit regularly and replace products that you have used. This prevents the unpleasant surprise of discovering that your emergency supplies are empty when you need them.

Having a period kit is not a sign that something is wrong. It is simply a practical way of preparing for a normal part of the menstrual cycle.

Whether you are at work, school, college, or travelling, being prepared can give you greater confidence and reduce stress when your period arrives unexpectedly.
